---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { AUTHOR, SITE_URL, GITHUB_URL, LINKEDIN_URL, PROFILES, CONTACT_EMAIL } from '../consts';

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Person',
  name: AUTHOR,
  url: SITE_URL,
  description: 'Data scientist making interactive maps of large text collections.',
  email: `mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`,
  sameAs: PROFILES,
};
---

<Base title="About" description="Who Steven Fazzio is and what he does." jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <h1>About</h1>
  <p>
    I'm Steven Fazzio. I'm a data scientist, but the through-line of my work is closer to
    "storyteller who codes." These days that mostly means making maps of large text collections:
    take a corpus (every game on Steam, five years of research papers, a video store's rental
    catalog), embed it, lay it out in two dimensions, and label the regions so you can actually
    explore the thing. If that sounds abstract, the <a href="/projects/">projects page</a> is the
    fastest way to see what I mean.
  </p>
  <p>
    I've worked at tech startups on marketing, data science, and engineering teams. I wrote my
    first (very small) language model about twenty years ago and have been hooked on computing with
    natural language ever since. My current interests sit at the intersection of NLP, embeddings,
    and data visualization, with a growing conviction that interactive visualization is a narrative
    medium: a good map isn't a chart, it's a place to wander around in.
  </p>
  <p>
    This site is the canonical home for my work. Every project gets a write-up here, the
    interactive artifacts live at subpaths of this domain, and everything is available by
    <a href="/rss.xml">RSS</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    I'm available for consulting through Fazzio Consulting. If you have a large pile of text and
    questions about what's inside it, <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>get in touch</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    You can also find me on <a rel="me" href={GITHUB_URL}>GitHub</a> and
    <a rel="me" href={LINKEDIN_URL}>LinkedIn</a>.
  </p>
</Base>
